WEBVTT

1
00:01.400 --> 00:05.510
This is how the Db2 for i MCP Server fits together.

2
00:05.560 --> 00:09.910
A client asks, the server checks the request against your policy,

3
00:09.960 --> 00:12.470
a driver talks to Db2 for i,

4
00:12.520 --> 00:15.650
and the IBM i decides what the user may read.

5
00:16.750 --> 00:18.700
There are two ways to run it.

6
00:18.750 --> 00:23.260
Over standard I/O, the client starts the server on your own machine:

7
00:23.310 --> 00:27.820
Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor and VS Code.

8
00:27.870 --> 00:31.553
Over HTTP, one server is shared by a team,

9
00:31.603 --> 00:33.687
and remote clients connect to it:

10
00:33.737 --> 00:39.980
claude.ai connectors, Claude for Excel, Cursor and Claude Code over HTTP,

11
00:40.030 --> 00:41.880
and your own agents.

12
00:42.980 --> 00:45.810
HTTP has three auth modes.

13
00:45.860 --> 00:51.463
None is for local development, and the server refuses to start it on a public address.

14
00:51.513 --> 00:55.117
Token mode uses one shared secret and one service account,

15
00:55.167 --> 00:56.770
for internal services.

16
00:56.820 --> 01:02.530
Required, the default, makes every user sign in with their own IBM i profile.

17
01:02.580 --> 01:05.330
With the built-in OAuth 2.1 server,

18
01:05.380 --> 01:08.930
claude.ai or Cursor open a sign-in page,

19
01:08.980 --> 01:11.810
the server tests the credentials on the IBM i,

20
01:11.860 --> 01:15.250
and the client gets a token, with a seven-day refresh.

21
01:15.300 --> 01:21.150
Nobody shares a service profile, and object authority and exit programs still apply.

22
01:22.450 --> 01:26.120
Three drivers, the same rules on all of them.

23
01:26.170 --> 01:28.320
ODBC is the default.

24
01:28.370 --> 01:31.680
It uses the IBM i Access ODBC Driver,

25
01:31.730 --> 01:35.360
needs no Java, and connects to the host servers.

26
01:35.410 --> 01:39.920
JT400 is the Java option, if you prefer JDBC.

27
01:39.970 --> 01:42.560
Mapepire runs over SSH:

28
01:42.610 --> 01:46.080
when only port 22 is open, the server logs in,

29
01:46.130 --> 01:49.040
starts Mapepire in the session and uses it.

30
01:49.090 --> 01:55.100
No daemon and no admin install, but the first query of a session takes a few seconds.

31
01:56.400 --> 02:00.350
A profile is one system, or one part of one.

32
02:00.400 --> 02:08.110
Use profiles for development, test and production, or for the countries and companies in one ERP installation.

33
02:08.160 --> 02:12.430
Two profiles can point at the same IBM i with different libraries,

34
02:12.480 --> 02:15.870
each with its own credentials, driver and allowlist.

35
02:15.920 --> 02:18.217
The first profile is the default.

36
02:18.267 --> 02:21.577
With more than one, every tool gets a system argument,

37
02:21.627 --> 02:24.730
and each sign-in is bound to one system.

38
02:26.030 --> 02:30.220
Run it with npx, install it globally with npm,

39
02:30.270 --> 02:32.140
or use the Docker image.

40
02:32.190 --> 02:35.127
Pin a version in production instead of latest.

41
02:35.177 --> 02:39.340
The image runs as a non-root user and comes in two targets:

42
02:39.390 --> 02:42.620
ODBC, which is amd64 only,

43
02:42.670 --> 02:47.020
and JT400, which also builds on arm64.

44
02:47.070 --> 02:49.240
Both can use Mapepire.

45
02:50.540 --> 02:54.190
For a team, put it behind HTTPS.

46
02:54.300 --> 02:57.650
One tested setup is AWS Fargate.

47
02:57.700 --> 03:01.850
A load balancer with a web application firewall takes the traffic.

48
03:01.900 --> 03:09.130
The server runs as a single task in a private subnet and reaches the IBM i over a site-to-site VPN.

49
03:09.180 --> 03:14.730
Secrets Manager holds the secrets, and EFS keeps sign-ins across restarts.

50
03:14.780 --> 03:19.050
Run one task, because authorization codes live in memory.

51
03:19.100 --> 03:21.850
Add the task's own address to the allowed hosts,

52
03:21.900 --> 03:23.690
or the health check fails.

53
03:23.740 --> 03:27.010
And raise the idle timeout for long queries.

54
03:27.060 --> 03:29.350
Nothing new runs on the IBM i.

55
03:30.850 --> 03:33.200
Security comes in layers.

56
03:33.250 --> 03:36.480
An SQL validator only accepts queries.

57
03:36.530 --> 03:39.360
The connection itself is read-only.

58
03:39.410 --> 03:42.960
IBM i parses each statement before it runs.

59
03:43.010 --> 03:46.000
A library allowlist limits what can be read,

60
03:46.050 --> 03:48.907
and masking hides sensitive columns.

61
03:48.957 --> 03:53.040
A timeout cancels a runaway statement on the IBM i itself,

62
03:53.090 --> 03:56.240
results are capped at a thousand rows by default,

63
03:56.290 --> 03:58.853
and every tool call goes to an audit log,

64
03:58.903 --> 04:00.987
with the SQL hashed.

65
04:01.037 --> 04:04.320
The last line of defense is the one you already have:

66
04:04.370 --> 04:07.580
the user's own authority on the IBM i.

67
04:09.080 --> 04:11.670
The model does better with context.

68
04:11.720 --> 04:15.670
In YAML, you describe tables, status codes and joins,

69
04:15.720 --> 04:22.150
write rules the model reads first, and turn vetted queries into named tools with bound parameters.

70
04:22.200 --> 04:28.310
validate_query lets IBM i check the model's SQL against the catalog before it runs,

71
04:28.360 --> 04:33.970
and a failed statement comes back with the same cause and recovery text you know from the green screen.

72
04:36.420 --> 04:39.970
It's open source, and every setting is in the docs.

73
04:40.020 --> 04:44.190
Start with npx mcp-server-db2i.
